nodecode-sealed is a rewrite of kailashrajh's kailashrajh/hermes-sealed:

https://github.com/kailashrajh/hermes-sealed
commit 9ab8bd2e6c81d055905e9558358177fca2cee5e2
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Kailash Rajh (LICENSE)

The plugin handed Hermes one SecretSource: a store file of AES-256-GCM
ciphertext under a raw 32-byte key file, both written 0600, opened at startup
and returned to the runtime as whole variables, with a `hermes sealed` command
to make, fill, list, inspect and rotate it. This rewrite keeps the format byte
for byte — the `SEALED1` header as the AEAD's associated data, the base64url
payload of nonce, ciphertext and tag, the dotenv body inside it, the same
quote-and-escape rules — so an existing store opens here unchanged, and it keeps
the original's posture: a read creates nothing, `list` answers names and
lengths and never a value, the key is never overwritten by a second `init`, and
a rotation keeps the previous key as a `.bak` beside it. What the original did
through the harness's secret-source seam, the cell does in its start clause: the
store is read once and its variables are put into Nodecode's own environment,
and the stop clause puts back exactly what each variable held, which the
original had no need of because it answered a process that was already
starting. The cipher is ironclad's AES-256-GCM, already in the image, instead
of the Python `cryptography` package, and the Python is gone: the four tools,
`/sealed`, `(help :sealed)` and the `(sealed:…)` verbs are the interface
instead of the `hermes sealed` command line. What was left out: the original's
`enabled` switch, which here is the section's own presence; its
`config_schema`/`remediation` hooks, which are Hermes's contract and not a
cell's; and the quiet value entry, so a value typed to `/sealed set` or sent to
`sealed_set` rides the transcript where the original's `getpass` prompt did not.
